BEGIN_HYPERDESC
<h1>Reentry breakup &mdash; Delta-glider</h1>
<p>The same hopeless entry, flown by a 24-tonne glider instead of a 91-tonne
orbiter: 100 km, <b>12 km/s</b>, 5.4&deg; below the horizon, belly-first at
40&deg;.</p>

<p>This is the vessel the heating limit was measured against. An entry it can
survive &mdash; 90 km at 7.7 km/s, 2&deg; down &mdash; peaks at about 72% of the
limit and loses nothing, which is the answer it should give. This one does not:
the same limit, the same energy-per-square-metre and the same size rules take a
ship of 649 m&sup2; apart the way they take one of 2399 m&sup2; apart, because
every threshold is a share of the vessel's own hull rather than a number of
joules. Both collider areas are printed in the log when the session starts.</p>

<p>Worth flying next to the Atlantis scenario for exactly that reason: two ships
a factor of four apart in hull area and mass, one set of constants.</p>

<p>Needs <b>CollisionDetection</b> on the Launchpad's Modules tab and <b>"Damage
and failure simulation"</b> on the Parameters tab, under Vessel.</p>
END_HYPERDESC

